  "strategy": "The proposed strategy involves a refined gradient descent approach with accurate gradient computation for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Instead of using random perturbations, this method computes the gradient based on the dominant shift that causes the maximum overlap. Here's the structured plan:\n\n1. **Initialization**: Start with the `initial_h_values`, ensuring sum(h)*dx =1.\n2. **Iterative Optimization**:\n   - Compute the correlation (convolution) of h with (1-h) to find the maximum overlap shift (k_max).\n   - Construct a shifted version of h (h_shifted) corresponding to k_max.\n   - Compute the gradient considering both direct contributions (from h_shifted) and indirect contributions (from terms involving the same h[x] in other positions).\n   - Update h using the negative gradient direction with an adaptive learning rate.\n3. **Constraint Handling**: After each update, clip h to [0,1] and project to maintain the integral constraint.\n4. **Learning Rate Adaptation**: Adjust the learning rate based on progress, decaying it over time for fine adjustments.\n5. **Termination**: Stop when the time budget is reached, returning the best solution.\n\nThis approach leverages precise gradient information to efficiently minimize the maximum overlap, aiming for a tighter upper bound on C\u2085.",
